[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the piece that circles back to what you originally asked for. You asked for a parameter on [38;5;153mlogin[39m, and I want to
  resolve that explicitly rather than quietly replace it with a return value.

  [1mThird question — what role does a parameter on [38;5;153mlogin[39m itself play?[0m Since the server mints the ID, the [3mfirst[0m login can't
  receive one. But a returning user has a persisted ID from last time, and passing it in is meaningful: it lets the
  server correlate "this browser was user X before, and is now authenticating as Y."

  - [1mOptional third parameter carrying the previously stored ID[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, previousUserId)[39m. Honors your
    original request, gives the server continuity information it can't otherwise get, and being optional means the
    existing call site and the first-ever login both keep working. Cost: two IDs in play during one call, which needs a
    clear name so nobody confuses "who you were" with "who you are."
  - [1mNo new parameter on [38;5;153mlogin[39m; it only returns the ID.[0m Other forms take [38;5;153muserId[39m as a parameter, [38;5;153mlogin[39m doesn't. Simplest
    and smallest, but it doesn't do the thing you literally asked for, and the returning-user link is lost.
  - [1mParameter that's the [3mcurrent[0;1m user's ID[0m — not viable. The caller can't know it before authenticating; that's the
    chicken-and-egg we identified earlier.

  My recommendation is the first. It's the reading of your request that survives everything we've established, and the
  continuity data genuinely serves "track who logged in." Dropping it later is trivial; retrofitting it means changing
  the signature a second time.
